WEBVTT

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:57.740
<v Ira Glass>A while back, I was in a taxi. The driver was a man named Ali Youssef. He has this pretty music playing on the tape deck, though the tape was a little on the warbly side. So at some point I asked him, what is the music? What is that? And he said, well, that's the music from his country, which, if I remember right, was Somalia. We talked for a while. And then he says to me, that's my wife. I say, really? And he says, yeah. She's still back in Africa. She's a singer at weddings, and like that. And he was here, and she was there. And he would drive around all day in his taxi, listening to her sing, hour after hour, on the road, thinking about her, missing her.
<v Ira Glass>不久前，我坐了一次出租车。司机名叫阿里·优素福。他的磁带播放器里放着优美的音乐，虽然磁带听起来有点走调。所以，在某个时候我问他，这是什么音乐？那是什么？他说，那是他祖国的音乐，如果我没记错的话，那是索马里。我们聊了一会儿。然后他对我说，那是我的妻子。我说，真的吗？他说，是的。她还在非洲。她是那里的婚礼歌手，诸如此类。他在这里，而她在那里。他整天开着出租车四处奔波，在路上听着她唱的歌，一小时又一小时，想着她，思念她。

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:40.620
<v Ira Glass>I picture him out there on the road right now, his tape playing, just one of the many people on the road right now. So much of the life of this country happens in cars. So much of it. Imagine for a moment that you could look down on all the streets and avenues and windy country roads and highways from above somehow, highway cloverleafs in a vast meadow spread before you. Each little car below its own little self-contained world, each one like its own little world when you go inside. In one, a high school sophomore tries to convince her boyfriend, Tony, and his friend, Milo, to buy tickets to the prom.
<v Ira Glass>我能想象出他此刻就在路上，播放着磁带，他只是此刻路上众多行人中的一个。这个国家很大一部分生活是在车里发生的。很大一部分。想象一下，如果你能从上方俯瞰所有的街道、大道、蜿蜒的乡间小路和高速公路，高速公路的立交桥就像一片铺展在你面前的巨大草地。下面每一辆小车都是一个独立的世界，当你走进去时，每一辆车都像是一个小世界。在其中一辆车里，一个高二女生正试图说服她的男友托尼和他的朋友米洛购买舞会门票。

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:41.990
<v Girl>Tony, pay for prom.
<v Girl>托尼，付钱参加舞会吧。

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:42.980
<v Tony>I'll get some money.
<v Tony>我会弄到钱的。

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:47.900
<v Ira Glass>Tony says he's broke, though he's about to spend over $100 on the paint job for the car.
<v Ira Glass>托尼说他没钱，尽管他打算花100多美元给车做喷漆。

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.560
<v Girl>I want to go to prom. I got a dress, too. I got three.
<v Girl>我想去参加舞会。我也买了礼服。我有三件。

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:52.430
<v Tony>You do.
<v Tony>你有吗。

00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:53.560
<v Ira Glass>Tony turns to his friend.
<v Ira Glass>托尼转向他的朋友。

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.420
<v Tony>Because I'd like to take her out.
<v Tony>因为我想带她出去。

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.970
<v Girl>No! Tony, you're going to regret this for this rest of his life if he doesn't.
<v Girl>不！托尼，如果你不这么做，你余生都会后悔的。

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.060
<v Milo>Yeah, you would?
<v Milo>是啊，你会吗？

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:00.330
<v Girl>Yeah.
<v Girl>是啊。

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:04.580
<v Milo>Trust me, because I'm regretting the date up to here.
<v Milo>相信我，因为我现在对这次约会后悔得要命。

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:24.740
<v Ira Glass>Meanwhile, in a four-door white 1991 Honda Accord, Lauren Burn is speeding through San Francisco. It's part of her job. Her trunk's all smashed, tied down with rope. She's been rear-ended twice. The car floor is cluttered with empty Mountain Dew bottles and discarded packs of cigarettes. On the door of the car, a sign's attached, "Lickety-Split Messengers."
<v Ira Glass>与此同时，在一位四门白色1991年本田雅阁车里，劳伦·伯恩正在旧金山超速行驶。这是她工作的一部分。她的后备箱撞坏了，用绳子捆着。她已经被追尾两次了。车内地板上堆满了空的山露饮料瓶和丢弃的烟盒。车门上贴着一个标志：“Lickety-Split 快递”。

00:02:24.740 --> 00:03:20.960
<v Lauren Burn>Whoa, red light. Every car's relationship to me is definitely they don't like me. They hate me because I think they're really scared and paranoid about, like, driving with no hands, talking on my radio, and next, at the same time, trying to write something down, trying to look for an address. And I look at it as a big video game. You have so much time to pick something up and drop something off in this time. And then you have all these obstacles in between, kind of like Paperboy, like the old video game. You know what I'm saying? Yeah, I've hit a few fire hydrants and a few people and a few different cars. And you get points taken off for that, I must say. Dude, I totally am going the wrong way.
<v Lauren Burn>哇，红灯。每辆车对我的态度肯定都是他们不喜欢我。他们讨厌我，因为我认为他们真的很害怕，对那种不把手放在方向盘上、对着无线电说话，同时又试图写下东西、寻找地址的驾驶方式感到神经质。我把它看作是一个大型电子游戏。你在这一段时间里有这么多时间去接东西和送东西。然后在中间你有所有这些障碍，有点像《派送员》，就像那个老式电子游戏。你知道我在说什么吗？是的，我撞过几个消防栓、几个人和几辆不同的车。我必须说，你会因为这些被扣分。伙计，我完全走错路了。

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:37.620
<v Ira Glass>In a year-old Chrysler 300M, Doris Kelly drives from a long day at work downtown, towards church, playing the CD that she plays every night after work on the way home, one her pastor recommended, one they play in church, called Spirit Songs .
<v Ira Glass>在一辆一年前买的克莱斯勒300M车里，多丽丝·凯利在市中心忙碌了一天后开车去教堂，播放着她每天下班回家路上都会听的CD，那是她的牧师推荐的，他们在教堂里也放，名字叫《灵歌》。

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:43.990
<v Ira Glass>[WORSHIP MUSIC]
<v Ira Glass>[礼拜音乐]

00:03:43.990 --> 00:04:26.090
<v Doris Kelly>People don't have peace. People don't have any joy. And they need that. And you see that driving on the expressways and driving on the streets. And people, you know, they're cutting in and out in front of you all the time, and honking horns and yelling at you, cursing and stuff. And if you listen to this kind of tape, you're calm, you're relaxed. You stop. You let people in. To me, it's equal to a martini that someone would go home and have a martini, relax.
<v Doris Kelly>人们没有平静。人们没有任何喜悦。他们需要那个。你在高速公路上和街道上开车时就能看到这一点。人们，你知道，他们一直都在你面前加塞，按喇叭，对你大喊大叫，咒骂等等。如果你听这种磁带，你会很冷静，很放松。你会停下来。你会让别人先行。对我来说，这等同于一杯马提尼，有人回家喝杯马提尼，放松一下。

00:04:26.090 --> 00:04:35.230
<v Man>You look like you need a drink, she said. He sat with his head in his hands, in a pale bleached oak armchair beneath a pastel seascape framed in ash.
<v Man>“你看上去需要喝一杯，”她说。他双手抱头坐在那里，坐在一张铺着白色漂白橡木扶手椅上，下方是一幅镶在白蜡木框里的淡色海景画。

00:04:35.230 --> 00:04:48.920
<v Ira Glass>In a navy blue Toyota Celica, Marlene Harris listens to one of the books on tape that she's in the middle of, a science fiction novel called Darwin's Radio. She loves books on tape.
<v Ira Glass>在海军蓝丰田赛利卡车里，玛琳·哈里斯正在听她读到一半的有声读物，那是一部名为《达尔文电台》的科幻小说。她很喜欢有声读物。

00:04:48.920 --> 00:05:04.560
<v Man>The secondary mucus plug seems to be in position. There was no trauma, no bleeding. The separation was textbook, if anybody has bothered to write a textbook about this sort of thing. The hospital did a quick biopsy. It's definitely a first-stage Shiva rejection.
<v Man>次级粘液栓似乎就位了。没有外伤，没有出血。分离是教科书式的，如果有人费心写过关于这种事情的教科书的话。医院做了一个快速活检。这绝对是第一阶段的湿婆排斥反应。

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:28.490
<v Marlene Harris>I'm wonder how this is going to come out. I'm thinking that the government's wrong and that Kaye Lang is right, and that her baby's going to be born alive. I do not think it's going to cause massive diseases. But I have a feeling they may start a colony, the mothers and fathers of these children.
<v Marlene Harris>我不知道这会如何发展。我在想政府是错的，而凯·朗是对的，她的孩子会活着出生。我不认为这会导致大规模的疾病。但我有一种预感，他们可能会建立一个殖民地，这些孩子的母亲和父亲们。

00:05:28.490 --> 00:05:32.690
<v Man>Let's hope. We'll do more tests in a few months.
<v Man>希望如此。我们几个月后再做更多测试。

00:05:32.690 --> 00:06:16.260
<v Marlene Harris>I'm partly in another world, not the parts that need to be driving a car. But I get very involved in the stories. I often listen to very involved things. I'm in the middle of a 20-book historical fiction series that takes place during the Napoleonic Wars, and takes place in the British navy. And I'm about to start book 14. And I talk about going out for a walk with Jack and Steven, who are the two main characters of this series, because by the time it's over I will have spent something like 300 hours with these guys.
<v Marlene Harris>我有一部分进入了另一个世界，不是那些需要开车的部分。但我非常沉浸在故事中。我经常听一些非常深入的东西。我正在读一个20本书的系列历史小说，故事发生在拿破仑战争期间，在英国海军中。我快要开始读第14本了。我谈论和杰克与史蒂文去散步，他们是这个系列的主角，因为等它结束时，我大概会花上300个小时和这些家伙在一起。

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:45.690
<v Ira Glass>If you're on the road right now in your car, listening to the radio, take a look inside the cars around you, each one its own little bubble, its own tiny subculture on wheels, glass and steel worlds rolling down the street alongside each other. Today on our radio program, we take you inside those other cars on the road. From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>如果你现在正在车里开车，听着收音机，看看你周围的车，每一辆都是一个独立的气泡，一个车轮上的微型亚文化，玻璃和钢铁的世界并排在街上滚动。今天在我们的广播节目中，我们带你进入路上那些其他的车里。来自芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:06:45.690 --> 00:07:24.640
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, we have lots of tape recorded inside moving vehicles, plus original stories by Nick Hornby and David Sedaris. We have parents and kids. We have longtime buddies drinking beer and cruising the neighborhood on a Sunday morning. We have drivers who save people, and drivers who do not want to be saved. The entire American road system is laid out before us, and let's start right in Chicago, on Chicago Avenue, where the Number 66 bus runs.
<v Ira Glass>在今天的节目中，我们有很多在移动车辆中录制的磁带，还有尼克·霍恩比和大卫·塞达里斯的原创故事。我们有父母和孩子。我们有老友在周日早晨喝着啤酒在社区里兜风。我们有拯救他人的司机，也有不想被拯救的司机。整个美国道路系统都在我们面前展开，让我们从芝加哥开始，在66路公交车经过的芝加哥大道上。

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:27.840
<v Linnel Peterson>Come on, boo, come on. How you doing? Come along, now.
<v Linnel Peterson>快点，宝贝，快点。你好吗？来吧，现在。

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:29.330
<v Woman>Hello.
<v Woman>你好。

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:31.040
<v Linnel Peterson>See, you made me miss my light.
<v Linnel Peterson>看，你害我错过了绿灯。

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:31.520
<v Woman>I'm sorry.
<v Woman>对不起。

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:32.970
<v Linnel Peterson>But that's OK.
<v Linnel Peterson>没关系。

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:35.390
<v Woman>[UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].
<v Woman>[模糊的短语]。

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:38.720
<v Linnel Peterson>We want to make sure we OK, though.
<v Linnel Peterson>我们要确保我们没事。

00:07:38.720 --> 00:08:07.950
<v Ira Glass>Let's stay here for a while. Linnel Peterson is 40, outgoing. The route she drives, Chicago Avenue, heads from the point where the City of Chicago touches Lake Michigan on the eastern edge of the city out to the western-most edge of the city, a route that is six miles in each direction, so long and slow to drive that in an eight-hour shift she only drives it back and forth four times. She's been a bus driver for six years.
<v Ira Glass>我们在这待一会儿吧。林内尔·彼得森40岁，性格外向。她驾驶的路线，芝加哥大道，从芝加哥市触及密歇根湖的东边缘一直到城市的最西端，每一方向都是六英里，开车又慢又长，所以在八小时的班次中，她只能来回跑四次。她做公交车司机已经六年了。

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:23.770
<v Linnel Peterson>When I was little, I didn't think you could back a bus up. There was a lot of things. I didn't really think about a bus. OK.
<v Linnel Peterson>我小的时候，没想过你可以倒着开公交车。有很多事情。我真的没想过公交车。好吧。

00:08:23.770 --> 00:08:55.550
<v Linnel Peterson>What's weird is when you're in your car, and you pull up to a bus stop, and you're getting ready to open the door, and you realize you're in your car. I'm serious. Or like, you know, somebody's running across the street, and you're starting to slow down because, mentally, you've got to pick 'em-- and I was like, OK. Ooh, big truck. He has flammables. I mean, he was flying, right behind us.
<v Linnel Peterson>奇怪的是，当你在车里，开车到公交车站，准备开门时，你意识到你是在自己的车里。我是认真的。或者像，你知道，有人在过马路，你开始减速，因为在精神上，你得去接他们——我就想，好的。哦，大卡车。他装有易燃物。我是说，他飞驰而过，就在我们后面。

00:08:55.550 --> 00:08:56.360
<v Linnel Peterson>Hi, Mommy.
<v Linnel Peterson>嗨，妈妈。

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:56.830
<v Woman>Hi baby!
<v Woman>嗨，宝贝！

00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:57.380
<v Linnel Peterson>How you doin?
<v Linnel Peterson>你好吗？

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:58.490
<v Woman>All right. And you?
<v Woman>挺好。你呢？

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:02.580
<v Linnel Peterson>Good. That's my mommy. That's my mother, right there.
<v Linnel Peterson>挺好。那是我的妈妈。就在那儿，那是我的母亲。

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.000
<v Woman>What's happening?
<v Woman>发生什么了？

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:05.070
<v Linnel Peterson>Nothing.
<v Linnel Peterson>没什么。

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:06.050
<v Woman>[INAUDIBLE]?
<v Woman>[听不清]？

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:06.950
<v Linnel Peterson>Yes, ma'am.
<v Linnel Peterson>是的，女士。

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:07.430
<v Woman>[INAUDIBLE].
<v Woman>[听不清]。

00:09:07.430 --> 00:09:29.680
<v Linnel Peterson>OK. Bye, Mommy. There are times that I've picked her up, and people get on the bus, and I'm like, that's my mommy, everybody. And they're laughing at me, because I still call her Mommy and I'm 40.
<v Linnel Peterson>好吧。再见，妈妈。有时候我接她，有人上车，我就说，那是我的妈妈，大家。他们会笑我，因为我40岁了还叫她妈妈。

00:09:29.680 --> 00:10:03.300
<v Linnel Peterson>Central Park, this is where I grew up at. Two blocks from here. My mom still lives down there. Watch your step. Have a good one. Two, exactly two blocks from here. And this is why I say, this is our bus route, going to school and-- because I went to Orr. And anything that we did, right here in this vicinity. Lydell.
<v Linnel Peterson>中央公园，这是我长大的地方。离这儿两个街区。我妈妈还住在下面。小心台阶。祝你有愉快的一天。两个，离这儿正好两个街区。这就是为什么我说，这是我们的公交路线，去上学——因为我去了奥尔高中。我们做的任何事，就在这附近。莱德尔。

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:29.010
<v Linnel Peterson>The grammar school I went to is a block down there. That's one of the neatest about being in a neighborhood. There are times when I see people who went to school with me, and they'll get on the bus. And I was like, I know you. Didn't you graduate from Orr? Or something like that. Like I'm saying, that's like a 23-year-ago thing. Central is next. Have a good one, baby.
<v Linnel Peterson>我上的小学在下面一个街区。这是在一个社区里最整洁的事情之一。有时候我看到和我一起上学的人，他们会上车。我就想，我认识你。你不是从奥尔高中毕业的吗？或者是类似的事情。就像我说的那样，那是23年前的事情了。下一站是中央街。祝你有愉快的一天，宝贝。

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:32.820
<v Woman>You, too. You too, darling.
<v Woman>你也是。你也是，亲爱的。

00:10:32.820 --> 00:11:11.080
<v Linnel Peterson>Thank you, baby. Watch your step. It used to be real nice where we grew up at. And it's like really drug-infested now. When we first moved over there, it was like a lot of Polish people that lived in the area. And that was about 30 years ago, when we first moved there, because I was about 10. Now it's only blacks. You might find a few.
<v Linnel Peterson>谢谢，宝贝。小心台阶。我们长大的地方曾经真的很不错。现在真的全是毒品。当我们刚搬到那里时，那个地区住着很多波兰人。那是大约30年前，当我们刚搬到那里时，因为我大概10岁。现在只有黑人了。你可能还能找到几个。

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:31.290
<v Linnel Peterson>OK, when we was in high school-- Hey, babe, step on out. Every so often, like when I pass Kostner, we used to stand outside and wait for the bus. And it would be so many of us, and we were kind of rowdy. The bus driver would kind of pass us up. It would be so funny. Not all the time, but-- Thank you, baby.
<v Linnel Peterson>好的，当我们上高中的时候——嘿，宝贝，走吧。每隔一段时间，比如当我经过科斯特纳的时候，我们过去常常站在外面等车。我们有很多人，而且有点吵闹。公交车司机过去常常会不停车。那会很有趣。不是一直这样，但是——谢谢，宝贝。

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:53.630
<v Linnel Peterson>So what happens is, when I get in that area, I kind of just start laughing. People are looking at me, like what's wrong with her? But people will usually ask me, you must be a nut to drive Chicago every day, because it's like a high-traffic area. But then, I think it's the fact that this is where I grew up at and I feel comfortable in it.
<v Linnel Peterson>所以发生的事情是，当我到达那个区域时，我就会开始笑。人们看着我，好像她怎么了？但人们通常会问我，你每天开芝加哥大道一定是疯了，因为那里是高交通区域。但后来，我想是因为这是我长大的地方，我在那里感到自在。

00:11:53.630 --> 00:12:00.770
<v Ira Glass>Linnel Peterson. She says on her days off, she tries to keep away from her route, Chicago Avenue, even though she lives right nearby.
<v Ira Glass>林内尔·彼得森。她说在她休息的日子里，她尽量远离她的路线，芝加哥大道，尽管她就住在附近。

00:12:00.770 --> 00:12:19.370
<v Linnel Peterson>I even avoid coming on the street, just the whole street. Because when I used to, I used to come on Chicago and I would be like, oh, there's a bus. Oh, he's out of place. So you just remember so much from the stuff that happens when you drive out here. Four lanes.
<v Linnel Peterson>我甚至避免来这条街，整条街。因为当我过去常来的时候，我过去常来芝加哥大道，我会想，哦，那有一辆公交车。哦，他不在位了。所以你会记得太多在你开车经过这里时发生的事情。四条车道。

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:39.350
<v Linnel Peterson>[MUSIC - "BABY ARE YENG" BY NANCY JACOBS & HER SISTER]
<v Linnel Peterson>[音乐 - 南希·雅各布斯及其姐妹的《BABY ARE YENG》]

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:53.210
<v Ira Glass>Across the ocean in England, cars are driving along the wrong side of the road, one of them an emerald green four-door car that we would call a Peugeot, but that they would call a Puh-joh. It's driven by Nick Hornby.
<v Ira Glass>在大洋彼岸的英国，汽车在道路的另一侧行驶，其中一辆是翡翠绿色的四门车，我们会称之为标致，但他们会称之为“Puh-joh”。它由尼克·霍恩比驾驶。

00:12:53.210 --> 00:13:24.110
<v Nick Hornby>So it's a lovely, sunny, crisp London Sunday morning, and I'm in the car with my son, Danny. Danny's six and autistic. And he loves the car. It really is a bubble to him, and nobody can burst it. When he's strapped into the backseat, he's safe. No other kids can get in his face, like they do out in the world where people fly at him like asteroids towards a spacecraft. And nobody will make him eat food he doesn't want to eat, and the side window is a videotape that never needs changing.
<v Nick Hornby>所以这是一个可爱、阳光明媚、干爽的伦敦周日早晨，我和我儿子丹尼在车里。丹尼六岁，患有自闭症。他很喜欢车。对他来说，它真的是一个气泡，没人能戳破它。当他被系在后座上时，他是安全的。没有其他孩子能凑到他脸上，不像在外面那个人们像小行星撞向航天器一样飞向他的世界。而且没人会强迫他吃他不想吃的食物，侧窗是一个永远不需要更换的录像带。

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:46.300
<v Nick Hornby>He like to know where he's going, though, so he's memorized all the significant routes. This is a kid who only occasionally remembers that the sequence beginning ready and steady is completed by the word "go," but somehow-- and these somehows constitute the enduring mystery of autism-- he's managed to construct a mental street map of the entire London metropolitan area.
<v Nick Hornby>不过他喜欢知道他要去哪儿，所以他背下了所有重要的路线。这是一个偶尔才记得以“ready”和“steady”开头的序列是由“go”这个词完成的孩子，但不知何故——而这些“不知何故”构成了自闭症持久的谜团——他成功地构建了整个大伦敦地区的心理街道地图。

00:13:46.300 --> 00:14:24.340
<v Nick Hornby>The route to school is OK, because he likes school. The route to grandma's house is OK, too, not only because he likes grandma, but because she lives 50-odd miles from London, so he gets to stay in the car longer. The route that's not OK is the route to the park. The park's too close to home, which means that the journey's over before it's properly begun. And someone, a bad person, his dad, will make him get out of the car. But it's a lovely, sunny, crisp Sunday morning. We're going to the park.
<v Nick Hornby>去学校的路线是可以的，因为他喜欢学校。去外婆家的路线也可以，不仅因为他喜欢外婆，还因为她住在离伦敦50多英里远的地方，所以他可以在车里待得更久。不去公园的路线是不行的。公园离家太近了，这意味着旅程还没正式开始就已经结束了。而且有人，一个坏人，他的爸爸，会让他下车。但这是一个可爱、阳光明媚、干爽的周日早晨。我们要去公园。

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:57.020
<v Nick Hornby>He starts to yell at the top of Delancey street in Camden when we don't turn left into Albany Street. That left turn he sees as his last chance. School was ruled out 10 minutes ago when we didn't turn into Liverpool Road after crossing Holloway Road at Drayton Park. See, Albany Street takes you onto Houston Road, and Houston Road leads eventually to the motorway and grandma's house. But from Delancey Street, we go straight across into Regent's Park Road on our way to Primrose Hill. Doesn't that sound nice, Primrose Hill? Not to Danny it doesn't.
<v Nick Hornby>当我们不在卡姆登的德兰西街尽头左转进入奥尔巴尼街时，他开始大叫。他把那个左转视为他最后的机会。学校在10分钟前就被排除了，因为我们在穿过德雷顿公园的霍洛威路后没有转入利物浦路。你看，奥尔巴尼街带你进入休斯顿路，休斯顿路最终通向高速公路和外婆家。但从德兰西街，我们径直穿过进入摄政公园路，前往樱草花山。那听起来不错，不是吗，樱草花山？对丹尼来说可不是。

00:14:57.020 --> 00:15:28.950
<v Nick Hornby>The yells get louder when we stop, and reach a sweat-inducing pitch when I open his door. Come on, Dan, I say, in my best fun voice. We're going to the park, the swings, the seesaw. He just turns the yellometer up to 11. I try to lead him out by the hand, but he snatches it away and grabs hold of something, the seat belt, anything that will anchor him inside. So we're fighting, the car and I, for custody of this small boy. The car has one end of Danny, and I have the other. The three of us are like a warring family in a TV soap.
<v Nick Hornby>当我们停车时，喊叫声变得更大，当我打开他的车门时，达到了让人出汗的音高。快点，丹，我用我最好的快乐声音说。我们要去公园，去荡秋千，玩跷跷板。他只是把“喊叫计”调到了11。我试图牵着他的手把他领出来，但他猛地抽走手，抓住任何东西，安全带，任何能把他固定在里面的东西。所以我们正在战斗，车和我，争夺这个小男孩的监护权。车抓住了丹尼的一端，我抓住了另一端。我们三个就像电视肥皂剧里交战的家庭。

00:15:28.950 --> 00:16:01.860
<v Nick Hornby>I end up dragging my son out by his ankles. A couple look at us as they walk past. They don't say anything, but one day I'm sure someone's going to report me and I'll be arrested. There's a certain irony to this. I learned to drive at the age of 41, entirely because of Danny. I didn't really want to learn, and I have a mild phobia about driving. But public transport, which had served me well all my adult London life, was becoming less and less fun.
<v Nick Hornby>我最终不得不拽着我儿子的脚踝把他拖出来。一对夫妇在走过时看着我们。他们什么也没说，但我敢肯定总有一天会有人举报我，我会被逮捕。这其中有一种讽刺意味。我在41岁时学会了开车，完全是因为丹尼。我并不真的想学，而且我对开车有轻微的恐惧症。但公共交通，在我整个成年伦敦生活中一直服务得很好，变得越来越没意思了。

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:22.080
<v Nick Hornby>Danny loves going on trains and buses, of course, but sometimes he didn't want to get off when I wanted to get off, and sometimes he wanted to get off before our stop, and sometimes he decided that he didn't want to wear any trousers on the top deck of the number 19. And though I won all these battles of will, because I'm bigger than him, I wasn't always in the mood to fight them in public.
<v Nick Hornby>丹尼当然喜欢坐火车和公交车，但有时我想下车时他不想下车，有时他想在我们到站前下车，有时他决定不想在19路车的顶层穿任何裤子。虽然我赢得了所有这些意志的较量，因为我比他大，但我并不总是想在公共场合进行这些较量。

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:48.910
<v Nick Hornby>They can be Pyrrhic victories anyway, these fights with autistic kids. Danny's best friend and classmate, Toby, once kicked up such a fuss about having his haircut at the hairdresser's that he had to be held down by his mom and his nanny, at which point a woman ran over and started pummeling them both with her fists. Sometimes I silently dare someone to say or do something, just so I can tell them why I'm having to be so cruel, and hopefully make them feel terrible in the process.
<v Nick Hornby>这些与自闭症儿童的争斗有时可能是皮洛士式的胜利。丹尼最好的朋友兼同学托比，曾经在理发店剪头发时闹得天翻地覆，以至于他不得不被他的妈妈和保姆按住，这时一个女人跑过来，开始用拳头猛击他们两个。有时我默默地希望有人说点什么或做点什么，这样我就可以告诉他们我为什么要这么残忍，并希望让他们在这个过程中感到糟糕。

00:16:48.910 --> 00:17:23.990
<v Nick Hornby>So anyway, I took driving lessons for 18 months, and slowly, slowly overcame my fear. And nothing, I felt, after every awful, jabbering, fear-filled 60-minute lesson, could've demonstrated more dramatically how much I love my son. I passed my test first time, and I bought a little four-door Peugeot that I thought he'd love, so I could drive him places like the park. And is he grateful? Is he? Hell. He's holding onto his seat belt for dear life and screaming while I try to pull him backwards onto the pavement.
<v Nick Hornby>总之，我学了18个月的驾驶课，慢慢地，慢慢地克服了我的恐惧。在每一节糟糕的、喋喋不休的、充满恐惧的60分钟课程之后，我觉得没有什么能比这更能戏剧性地证明我有多爱我的儿子。我第一次就通过了考试，我买了一辆小四门标致，我想他会喜欢的，这样我就可以带他去像公园这样的地方。他感激吗？他感激吗？见鬼。他拼命抓住安全带尖叫，而我试图把他向后拉到人行道上。

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:45.410
<v Nick Hornby>Eventually, he can hold on no longer, and I lift him out and put him down. And after a brief pause during which he recovers his composure and stops yelling, he roars off towards the park gates, because another thing Danny's forgotten until this very second is that he loves the park. He loves the swings and the seesaw, and spinning round and round on the grass until he's dizzy.
<v Nick Hornby>最终，他再也抓不住了，我把他抱出来放下。在他短暂的停顿，恢复冷静并停止喊叫后，他向公园大门咆哮而去，因为丹尼直到这一秒才想起的另一件事是他热爱公园。他喜欢荡秋千、玩跷跷板，在草地上不停地转圈，直到头晕目眩。

00:17:45.410 --> 00:18:01.240
<v Nick Hornby>And it's a lovely, sunny, crisp Sunday morning. And hey, there's an empty swing. And literally within 10 seconds, he's full of smiles and happy anticipation. And there's no trace whatsoever in his face of the ankle-pulling trauma to which he was so recently and cruelly subjected.
<v Nick Hornby>那是一个可爱、阳光明媚、干爽的周日早晨。嘿，有一个空的秋千。字面上在10秒之内，他就充满了微笑和快乐的期待。他脸上完全没有刚才被我残酷对待而留下的脚踝拉伤的痕迹。

00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:25.000
<v Nick Hornby>And I want to find the couple who may or may not have had a disapproving look on their faces when they saw me committee awful acts of violence, and show them just how joyful he is now. But of course they're not around, which is maybe just as well, because in a while I'm going to have to find a way to get him out of this swing.
<v Nick Hornby>我想找到那对可能或可能没有在看到我实施可怕暴力行为时露出反感表情的夫妇，向他们展示他现在有多快乐。但当然他们不在附近，这也许是件好事，因为过一会儿我就得想办法让他从秋千上下来。

00:18:25.000 --> 00:18:36.740
<v Ira Glass>Nick Hornby is the author of High Fidelity and About a Boy .
<v Ira Glass>尼克·霍恩比是《高保真》和《关于一个男孩》的作者。

00:18:36.740 --> 00:18:54.740
<v Ira Glass>Back on this side of the Atlantic again, though not very far inland, on Cape Cod. In a green Volvo sedan, Jay Allison drives his daughter, Lily, to ice skating lessons. She's 12, which means-- surprise!-- in the car they do not agree about what radio station to play.
<v Ira Glass>回到大西洋的这一边，虽然离内陆不远，在科德角。在一辆绿色沃尔沃轿车里，杰伊·艾利森开车送他的女儿莉莉去上滑冰课。她12岁，这意味着——惊喜！——在车里，他们对该放哪个电台意见不一致。

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:56.030
<v Lily>Are we going to be late?
<v Lily>我们要迟到了吗？

00:18:56.030 --> 00:19:00.080
<v Jay Allison>Uh, probably.
<v Jay Allison>呃，可能吧。

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:02.100
<v Lily>Leigh's going to kill me. She hates me already.
<v Lily>莉要杀了我。她已经讨厌我了。

00:19:02.100 --> 00:19:03.900
<v Jay Allison>Why does she hate you?
<v Jay Allison>为什么她讨厌你？

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:10.410
<v Lily>I don't know. Because we're always late.
<v Lily>我不知道。因为我们总是迟到。

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:11.940
<v Jay Allison>We're not always late.
<v Jay Allison>我们不总是迟到。

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:20.480
<v Lily>We usually are.
<v Lily>我们通常是。

00:19:20.480 --> 00:19:22.900
<v Jay Allison>Oh, man.
<v Jay Allison>天哪。

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:26.380
<v Lily>Well, you never let me listen. It's not fair.
<v Lily>好吧，你从来不让我听。这不公平。

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:28.670
<v Jay Allison>I always let you listen.
<v Jay Allison>我总是让你听。

00:19:28.670 --> 00:19:30.470
<v Lily>Oh my god, are you serious?
<v Lily>天哪，你是认真的吗？

00:19:30.470 --> 00:19:33.330
<v Jay Allison>No, seriously. I've lost so many brain cells in this car thanks to you.
<v Jay Allison>不，我是说真的。多亏了你，我在车里损失了太多脑细胞。

00:19:33.330 --> 00:19:40.260
<v Lily>Oh, really?
<v Lily>哦，真的吗？

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:42.240
<v Jay Allison>See, there go a few.
<v Jay Allison>看，又损失了几个。

00:19:42.240 --> 00:19:42.730
<v Lily>There go a couple more.
<v Lily>又损失了几个。

00:19:42.730 --> 00:19:43.720
<v Jay Allison>Really.
<v Jay Allison>真的。

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:44.710
<v Lily>Really?
<v Lily>真的吗？

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:51.180
<v Jay Allison>It's hurting me. It's going into my brain and--
<v Jay Allison>这伤害了我。它进入我的大脑，然后——

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:53.610
<v Lily>It's going into your brain and hurting you?
<v Lily>它进入你的大脑伤害你？

00:19:53.610 --> 00:19:58.050
<v Jay Allison>Yes. Ow.
<v Jay Allison>是的。哎哟。

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:00.480
<v Lily>This is Santana. Shouldn't you be gaining?
<v Lily>这是桑塔纳。你不应该在增强吗？

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.840
<v Jay Allison>All right, I feel-- Gaining strength from Carlos?
<v Jay Allison>好吧，我觉得——从卡洛斯那里获得力量？

00:20:03.840 --> 00:20:16.720
<v Lily>Yep. I love [UNINTELLIGIBLE].
<v Lily>是的。我喜欢[听不清]。

00:20:16.720 --> 00:20:20.520
<v Jay Allison>OK, make it stop. Make it go away. No, no, no.
<v Jay Allison>好的，让它停下。让它消失。不，不，不。

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:22.470
<v Lily>That's OK.
<v Lily>没关系。

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:23.450
<v Jay Allison>Kill it.
<v Jay Allison>杀了它。

00:20:23.450 --> 00:20:26.380
<v Lily>Kill it.
<v Lily>杀了它。

00:20:26.380 --> 00:20:31.030
<v Jay Allison>Thank you.
<v Jay Allison>谢谢。

00:20:31.030 --> 00:20:35.540
<v Lily>I've always felt so bad with the Rose, like all those other stations, the good stations.
<v Lily>我一直对罗斯电台感觉很糟，就像所有那些其他电台，好的电台。

00:20:35.540 --> 00:20:36.680
<v Jay Allison>What about them?
<v Jay Allison>它们怎么了？

00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:39.530
<v Lily>What's so bad about them?
<v Lily>它们有什么不好的？

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:43.390
<v Jay Allison>There's no intelligent life on them.
<v Jay Allison>上面没有智慧生命。

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:46.350
<v Lily>Oh, geez.
<v Lily>哦，天哪。

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:50.130
<v Jay Allison>I'm going to short-circuit your radio so it will only play public radio.
<v Jay Allison>我要把你的收音机短路，这样它只能播放公共广播。

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:55.520
<v Lily>That's sad. I don't know what I'd do. I'd buy a new radio.
<v Lily>那太可悲了。我不知道我会做什么。我会买一个新的收音机。

00:20:55.520 --> 00:21:00.580
<v Jay Allison>There are lots of good shows on public radio.
<v Jay Allison>公共广播里有很多好节目。

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:03.470
<v Lily>OK, whatever.
<v Lily>好的，随便吧。

00:21:03.470 --> 00:21:06.920
<v Jay Allison>What. Ever.
<v Jay Allison>随。便。

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:11.310
<v Lily>That was cool, Dad. Do that again.
<v Lily>那太酷了，爸爸。再做一次。

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:31.410
<v Jay Allison>Thank you, Lil.
<v Jay Allison>谢谢，莉尔。

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:34.370
<v Lily>[UNINTELLIGIBLE].
<v Lily>[听不清]。

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:38.960
<v Jay Allison>What, do we get to talk for a few minutes, and then we have to-- It's like you're going to the surface for oxygen?
<v Jay Allison>什么，我们得聊几分钟，然后我们得——就像你要浮出水面呼吸氧气一样？

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:42.020
<v Lily>"Say My Name."
<v Lily>“说出我的名字”。

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:49.840
<v Lily>[MUSIC - "SAY MY NAME" BY DESTINY'S CHILD]
<v Lily>[音乐 - 天命真女的《SAY MY NAME》]

00:21:49.840 --> 00:21:52.330
<v Jay Allison>OK, make it stop it. It just hurt me.
<v Jay Allison>好的，让它停下。它刚刚伤害了我。

00:21:52.330 --> 00:21:53.280
<v Lily>I'll find a good one.
<v Lily>我会找一个好的。

00:21:53.280 --> 00:21:54.230
<v Lily>[MUSIC - "MARIA, MARIA" BY CARLOS SANTANA]
<v Lily>[音乐 - 卡洛斯·桑塔纳的《MARIA, MARIA》]

00:21:54.230 --> 00:21:57.330
<v Lily>Here, more Santana. Are you gaining cells?
<v Lily>给你，更多桑塔纳。你的细胞在增强吗？

00:21:57.330 --> 00:22:11.540
<v Jay Allison>Santana, Santana's gone over to the dark side a little bit.
<v Jay Allison>桑塔纳，桑塔纳稍微投向了黑暗面。

00:22:11.540 --> 00:22:15.980
<v Jay Allison>[MUSIC - "SHOW ME THE MEANING OF BEING LONELY" BY THE BACKSTREET BOYS]
<v Jay Allison>[音乐 - 后街男孩的《SHOW ME THE MEANING OF BEING LONELY》]

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:18.450
<v Lily>They did a remix.
<v Lily>他们做了一个混音版。

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:21.840
<v Jay Allison>A remix? They didn't like the first one?
<v Jay Allison>混音版？他们不喜欢第一版吗？

00:22:21.840 --> 00:22:25.620
<v Lily>It wasn't popular enough. It was number one for, like, two days.
<v Lily>不够流行。它在第一位待了大约两天。

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:27.040
<v Jay Allison>What was?
<v Jay Allison>什么是在第一位？

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:35.870
<v Lily>This song. It's like another Backstreet Boys song. And did you know that Kevin Richardson of the Backstreet Boys is engaged?
<v Lily>这首歌。就像另一首后街男孩的歌。你知道后街男孩的凯文·理查森订婚了吗？

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:37.730
<v Jay Allison>Did I know that? Of course I knew that.
<v Jay Allison>我知道吗？我当然知道。

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:38.700
<v Lily>Really?
<v Lily>真的吗？

00:22:38.700 --> 00:22:45.870
<v Jay Allison>Oh, sure. His birthday's coming up, isn't it? Kevin's?
<v Jay Allison>哦，当然。他的生日快到了，不是吗？凯文的？

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:50.860
<v Lily>Oh my god. You're so weird.
<v Lily>天哪。你太奇怪了。

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:53.500
<v Jay Allison>[SINGING] I want to love you, baby.
<v Jay Allison>[唱歌] 我想爱你，宝贝。

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:59.960
<v Lily>Oh, god. Please stop, please. Just let me hear this once song, and then you can--
<v Lily>哦，天哪。请停下，请。就让我听这首歌一次，然后你可以——

00:22:59.960 --> 00:23:03.640
<v Jay Allison>Yeah, no, I love this song.
<v Jay Allison>是的，不，我喜欢这首歌。

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:06.000
<v Lily>Aren't you embarrassed?
<v Lily>你不尴尬吗？

00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.960
<v Jay Allison>[SINGING] Everybody sing with me.
<v Jay Allison>[唱歌] 大家和我一起唱。

00:23:08.960 --> 00:23:10.430
<v Lily>Dad, stop. Stop it, Dad.
<v Lily>爸爸，停下。停下，爸爸。

00:23:10.430 --> 00:23:15.240
<v Jay Allison>What? So maybe the secret is I should start liking your music.
<v Jay Allison>什么？所以也许秘诀是我应该开始喜欢你的音乐。

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:17.170
<v Lily>No, that would be really bad.
<v Lily>不，那会很糟糕。

00:23:17.170 --> 00:23:17.650
<v Jay Allison>Yes.
