<v Ira Glass>Coming up, David Sedaris with a special message for young people about what happens when you get high and get into a car with your own mother. And adventures on the way to Hubcap City. That's in a minute, from Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，大卫·塞达里斯将给年轻人传达一个特别的信息，关于当你吸食毒品后和你自己的母亲一起上车会发生什么。还有通往轮毂盖城的冒险。一会儿就到，来自国际公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:47.030
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. It's often said that this country was rebuilt in the 20th century to accommodate automobiles. Because of cars, we have the suburbs, the malls, the highway system, much of the physical and commercial structure of this country. And so, if cars are so important, today's program is made up entirely of stories that happen in cars.
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。人们常说这个国家在20世纪是为了适应汽车而重建的。因为汽车，我们有了郊区、购物中心、高速公路系统，以及这个国家大部分的物理和商业结构。所以，如果汽车如此重要，今天的节目就完全由发生在车里的故事组成。

00:27:47.030 --> 00:28:08.830
<v Ira Glass>Outside Washington, DC, a couple times a week, in an '88 Mercury Grand Marquis that he inherited from his mother, Senator Conrad Burns of Montana picks up random passengers from Virginia, drives them into the city during his rush hour commute. Having people in the car means that he can drive in the fast lanes that are set aside for cars that are full. He told me that the conversations also let him know what issues are on people's minds.
<v Ira Glass>在华盛顿特区外，每周几次，在他从母亲那里继承的88年水星大侯爵车里，蒙大拿州的参议员康拉德·伯恩斯从弗吉尼亚州接载随机乘客，在上下班高峰期开车带他们进入市区。车里有人意味着他可以在那些留给满载车辆的快速车道上行驶。他告诉我，这些谈话也让他知道人们心中关心的问题。

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:35.000
<v Conrad Burns>Politicians, I think, have a big problem. They've never learned the lesson that you can't hear with your mouth open. You know, it's interesting what people talk about, especially-- But they'll always notice, sometimes they notice the Montana plates and they'll say, well, you're from Montana? And I said, yeah, I'm native. They ask what I do. And sometimes I sort of just don't answer or I give them some sort of a nondescript answer.
<v Conrad Burns>政客，我认为，有一个大问题。他们从未学到教训，那就是你不能在张着嘴的时候听。你知道，人们谈论的事情很有趣，尤其是——但他们总是会注意到，有时他们注意到蒙大拿的车牌，他们会说，哦，你来自蒙大拿？我说，是的，我是本地人。他们问我做什么。有时我只是不回答，或者给他们某种不具体的回答。

00:28:35.000 --> 00:28:39.960
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait. Is the nondescript answer-- Do you just say, I work in the government, something like that?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。这个不具体的回答是——你只是说，我在政府工作，类似这样吗？

