00:29:17.720 --> 00:30:07.780
<v Woman>Sometimes I do remember them. Actually, when I was in my early 20s, I was having a recurrent dream of my family being edible. I had dreamt once that my mother was a Fig Newton and that I was dating Mr. Nabisco. And I also recall dreaming that my father's feet were made of roast beef, and that he would sit in his usual chair and slice off pieces of his roast beef feet to feed us with.
<v Woman>有时我确实记得它们。事实上，我在20岁出头时，经常做梦梦见我的家人是可以食用的。我曾经梦见过我母亲是一个无花果牛顿饼干，而我正在和纳比斯克先生约会。我还记得梦见我父亲的脚是烤牛肉做的，他会坐在他平时坐的椅子上，切下他烤牛肉脚的碎片来喂我们。

00:30:07.780 --> 00:30:45.900
<v Ira Glass>On the other side of the country, where dreams come true, Los Angeles. Cars scoot along the 405 and the 10. When Rob Levine first came out west, he got his first Hollywood job in one of those cars, a mid-1980s BMW 5 series, big heavy car. He as the driver for a movie producer named Edgar Sherick, who was the head of ABC back in the '60s, went on to produce lots of films. Heartbreak Kid , The Stepford Wives, Mrs. Soffel , Woody Allen's first movie, Take the Money and Run. Driver, in this world, can be a job where you train to be a movie producer yourself, which Rob Levine wanted. So he not only took care of the car, he read every script, sat in on meetings, learned everything.
<v Ira Glass>在国家的另一端，梦想成真的地方，洛杉矶。汽车在405号和10号公路上穿梭。当罗伯·莱文第一次来到西部时，他在其中一辆车里得到了他的第一份好莱坞工作，那是一辆80年代中期的宝马5系，大型重型车。他是电影制片人埃德加·谢里克的司机，埃德加·谢里克在60年代是ABC的负责人，后来制作了很多电影。《心碎小子》、《斯戴福妻子》、《索菲夫人》、伍迪·艾伦的第一部电影《傻瓜大闹科学城》。在这个世界里，司机可以是一份让你训练自己成为电影制片人的工作，这也是罗伯·莱文想要的。所以他不仅照顾车，还读了每一个剧本，旁听了会议，学会了一切。

00:30:45.900 --> 00:31:03.970
<v Rob Levine>Part of the idea was that I would shadow him. I was expected to know everything he was reading. I was expected to know who he was calling and why. And he would ask me routinely, why am I calling this person? And not just to test me, because he honestly would slip his mind, and I would have to know.
<v Rob Levine>想法的一部分是我会跟随他。我被期望了解他在读的所有东西。我被期望知道他在给谁打电话以及为什么。他会习惯性地问我，为什么我要给这个人打电话？不仅仅是为了测试我，因为他真的会忘记，而我必须知道。

00:31:03.970 --> 00:31:07.930
<v Ira Glass>Wait. He would say, dial the phone for me, why am I calling?
