00:43:14.670 --> 00:43:26.220
<v Ira Glass>Some of the most important moments of people's lives happen in cars, though sometimes we are not aware that these are the most important moments in our lives as they're happening. David Sedaris tells this story.
<v Ira Glass>人们生活中一些最重要的时刻发生在车里，尽管有时我们并没有意识到这些是我们生命中发生的最重要的时刻。大卫·塞达里斯讲述了这个故事。

00:43:26.220 --> 00:44:01.810
<v David Sedaris>It wasn't anything I'd planned on, but at the age of 22, after dropping out of my second college and traveling back and forth across the country a few times, I found myself back in Raleigh, North Carolina, living in my parents' basement. After I'd spent six months waking at noon, getting high, and listening to the same Joni Mitchell record over and over again, my father called me into his den and told me to get out. He was sitting very formally in a big comfortable chair behind his desk. And I felt as though he was firing me from the job of being his son.
<v David Sedaris>这不是我计划中的任何事情，但在22岁时，在从我的第二所大学退学并在这个国家来回旅行了几次后，我发现自己回到了北卡罗来纳州的罗利，住在我父母的地下室里。在我花了六个月的时间在中午醒来、吸毒、反复听同一张琼尼·米切尔的唱片后，我父亲把我叫进他的书房，告诉我离开。他非常正式地坐在他书桌后一张舒适的大椅子里。我觉得他就像是在解雇我作为一个儿子的工作。

00:44:01.810 --> 00:44:33.650
<v David Sedaris>I'd seen this coming, and it honestly didn't bother me all that much. The way I saw it, being kicked out of the house was just what I needed if I was ever going to get back on my feet. Fine, I said. I'll go, but one day you'll be sorry. I had no idea what I meant by that. It just seemed like the sort of thing a person should say when they were told to leave.
<v David Sedaris>我预料到了这一点，它老实说并没有太困扰我。在我看来，被赶出家门正是我重新站起来所需要的。好的，我说。我会走的，但总有一天你会后悔的。我不知道我那是什么意思。那只是当一个人被告知要离开时应该说的那种话。

00:44:33.650 --> 00:45:00.780
<v David Sedaris>My sister, Lisa, had an apartment over by the university, and said I could come stay with her as long as I didn't bring my Joni Mitchell record. My mother offered to drive me over. And after a few bong hits, I took her up on it. It was a 15-minute ride across town, and on the way my mother and I listened to the rebroadcast of a radio call-in show, in which people phoned the host to describe the various birds gathered around their backyard feeders.
<v David Sedaris>我姐姐丽莎在大学那边有一套公寓，说我可以去和她住，只要我不带我的琼尼·米切尔唱片。我妈妈提议开车送我过去。在吸了几口大麻后，我接受了她的好意。那是一段穿过城镇15分钟的旅程，在路上，我和我妈妈听了一个广播电话节目的重播，人们打电话给主持人描述聚集在他们后院喂食器周围的各种鸟类。

00:45:00.780 --> 00:45:38.100
<v David Sedaris>Normally, the show came on in the morning, and it seemed strange to listen to it at night. The birds in question had gone to bed hours ago, and probably had no idea they were still being talked about. I thought about this and wondered if anyone back at the house was talking about me. To the best of my knowledge, no one had ever tried to describe my voice or the shape of my head. And it depressed me that I went unnoticed while people were willing to drop everything for a cardinal.
<v David Sedaris>通常，这个节目是在早上播放的，而在晚上听它看起来很奇怪。相关的鸟儿几小时前已经睡觉了，可能根本不知道还在谈论它们。我想到了这一点，并想知道家里是否有人在谈论我。据我所知，从来没有人试图描述我的声音或我的头型。这让我感到沮丧，因为我被忽视了，而人们却愿意为了红雀放下一切。

00:45:38.100 --> 00:46:26.870
<v David Sedaris>My mother pulled up in front of my sister's apartment. And when I opened the car door, she started to cry, which worried me because she normally didn't do things like that. It wasn't one of those, I'm going to miss you things, but something deeper and sadder than that. I wouldn't know it until months later, but my father had kicked me out of the house not because I was a bum, but because I was gay. Our little talk was supposed to be one of those defining moments that shape a person's adult life, but he'd been so uncomfortable with the most important word that he'd left it out completely, saying only, I think we both know why I'm doing this. I'd assumed it was because of the drugs, and had left it at that. I guess I could've nailed him down. I just hadn't seen the point.
<v David Sedaris>我妈妈停在我姐姐公寓前。当我打开车门时，她开始哭泣，这让我很担心，因为她通常不做那种事。那不是那种“我会想念你”的事，而是比那更深、更悲伤的东西。我直到几个月后才知道，我父亲赶我出家门不是因为我是一个流浪汉，而是因为我是同性恋。我们的小谈话本应该是塑造一个人成年生活的决定性时刻之一，但他对这个最重要的词感到如此不舒服，以至于他完全忽略了它，只说，我想我们都知道我为什么要这样做。我以为这是因为毒品，就这样算了。我想我本可以逼问他的。我只是没看到重点。

00:46:26.870 --> 00:46:55.320
<v David Sedaris>My mother assumed that I knew the truth, and it tore her up. Here was another defining moment, and again I missed it completely. She cried until it sounded as if she were choking. I'm sorry, she said. I'm sorry. I'm sorry. I'm sorry. I figured that within a few weeks, I'd have a job and some crummy little apartment. It didn't seem insurmountable, but my mother's tears made me worry that finding these things might be a little harder than I thought.
<v David Sedaris>我妈妈以为我知道真相，这让她心碎。这是另一个决定性时刻，我又完全错过了它。她哭得听起来好像她在窒息。对不起，她说。对不起。对不起。对不起。我以为几周内我就会有一份工作和一套破旧的小公寓。这看起来并非无法克服，但我妈妈的眼泪让我担心找到这些东西可能比我想象的要难一点。

00:46:55.320 --> 00:47:43.630
<v David Sedaris>Did she honestly think I was that much of a loser? Really, I said. I'll be fine. The car light was on, and I wondered what the passing drivers thought as they watched my mother sob. What kind of people did they think we were? Did they think she was one of those crybaby moms who fell apart every time someone chipped a coffee cup? Did they assume I'd said something to hurt her? Did they see us as just another crying mother and her stoned gay son, sitting in a station wagon and listening to a call-in show about birds? Or did they imagine, for just one moment, that we might be special?
<v David Sedaris>她真的认为我是那么失败的人吗？真的，我说。我会没事的。车里的灯亮着，我好奇当经过的司机看着我妈妈抽泣时在想什么。他们认为我们是什么样的人？他们认为她是那种每次有人碰坏咖啡杯就会崩溃的爱哭妈妈吗？他们以为我说了一些伤害她的话吗？他们是否把我们视为另一个哭泣的母亲和她吸了毒的同性恋儿子，坐在旅行车里听关于鸟类的电话节目？还是他们想象，哪怕只是一瞬间，我们可能是特别的？

00:47:43.630 --> 00:47:50.350
<v Ira Glass>Dave Sedaris' latest book is called Me Talk Pretty One Day.
<v Ira Glass>戴夫·塞达里斯的最新书名为《我谈得很漂亮的一天》。

