<v Man>Yeah, we have a T3 on Prospect. Do you want me to send that over?
<v Man>是的，我们在Prospect有一个T3。你想让我把它发过去吗？

00:49:03.910 --> 00:49:09.360
<v Kelly Keggers>Yeah, 10-4. Prospect, where the [BLEEP] is that?
<v Kelly Keggers>是的，10-4。Prospect，那玩意儿在哪儿啊？

00:49:09.360 --> 00:49:17.760
<v Ira Glass>Kelly Keggers wears dark blue pants and a button-down AAA service shirt. The dispatch computer on her dashboard lists AAA members in trouble.
<v Ira Glass>凯利·凯格斯穿着深蓝色的裤子和一件扣领AAA服务衬衫。她仪表盘上的调度电脑列出了遇到麻烦的AAA会员。

00:49:17.760 --> 00:49:38.950
<v Kelly Keggers>Well, all I do all day is go out and help people. It's like community service. People call when they're in need, if their car doesn't start, or if they've got a flat tire, or they run out of gas or something. If their car's not working, they call. It's all day.
<v Kelly Keggers>嗯，我整天做的就是出去帮助别人。这就像社区服务。当人们有需要时打电话，如果他们的车启动不了，或者如果他们轮胎瘪了，或者他们没油了之类的。如果他们的车不工作，他们就打电话。这是一整天。

00:49:38.950 --> 00:50:06.970
<v Kelly Keggers>And everytime I show up, people are like all, oh my god, you're here. It's like I'm all doing them a favor or something. It's a good deed job. Early in the morning, though, it's, like, jump-starts, but pretty much people lock their keys in their car. They're getting ready for work, and they're freaking out and-- Oh, here comes my member. What's happening with your car? It won't start?
<v Kelly Keggers>而且每次我出现，人们都像，天哪，你在这儿。这就像我在为他们做人情一样。这是一个善举工作。不过清晨，是那种，跳线启动，但基本上人们把钥匙锁在车里。他们准备去上班，他们吓坏了，而且——哦，我的会员来了。你的车怎么了？它不启动？

00:50:06.970 --> 00:50:16.260
<v Woman>Battery's dead. I keep switching the lights on because it--
<v Woman>电池没电了。我一直开着灯，因为它——

00:50:16.260 --> 00:50:38.850
<v Kelly Keggers>Her horn did-- like, you know, lights are on the blinker of your car, like if you put the blinker up or down? A lot of people complain about the new Hondas. They turn the blinker on during the day, they're coming home from, blah, blah, blah, whatever, and they leave their lights on all night long. And there's no buzzer or anything to tell them that their frickin' lights are on.
<v Kelly Keggers>她的喇叭确实——像，你知道，灯在你的车转向灯上，像如果你向上或向下拨转向灯？很多人抱怨新的本田。他们在白天开转向灯，他们从别的地方回家，等等，不管什么，他们把灯整晚都开着。而且没有任何蜂鸣器或其他东西告诉他们他们该死的灯开着。

00:50:38.850 --> 00:50:47.090
<v Kelly Keggers>So she's all pissed off about that. And she was worried about her battery, but it's a brand-new car. It's a 1999. Oh, wait. Let me get these calls.
<v Kelly Keggers>所以她对那个很生气。她担心她的电池，但这是一辆全新的车。它是1999年的。哦，等等。让我接这些电话。

00:50:47.090 --> 00:50:58.670
<v Man>I have three more service calls. Two of them are kind of a ways away, I think. We have this 6 at Third and Cargo. Three at Gillman, and Raymond and Redland.
<v Man>我有三个以上的服务电话。我想其中两个有点远。我们在第三和Cargo有6。Gillman有3，还有Raymond和Redland。

00:50:58.670 --> 00:51:04.790
<v Kelly Keggers>OK, I can do all three of those. That's cool. Go ahead and send them my way.
<v Kelly Keggers>好的，我可以做那三个。那很酷。继续把它们发给我。

00:51:04.790 --> 00:51:09.160
<v Man>10-4. Copy that.
<v Man>10-4。复制那个。

00:51:09.160 --> 00:51:19.910
<v Kelly Keggers>I love driving around without a boss in the car with me. I don't like to work with-- I'm not really much of a team player, I guess.
<v Kelly Keggers>我喜欢在没有老板在车里的情况下开车。我不喜欢和——我想我不是那种团队合作者。

00:51:19.910 --> 00:51:21.180
<v Man>On West 7th?
<v Man>在西7街？

00:51:21.180 --> 00:51:42.950
<v Kelly Keggers>I don't like to work with people that much. I just like to work by myself and do my own thing. I just get things done faster and better that way. I guess I can just concentrate by myself. I mean, I like helping people, though. I get a kick out of it. It's kind of a good feeling.
<v Kelly Keggers>我不喜欢和人合作那么多。我只是喜欢自己工作，做我自己的事。我那样做事更快更好。我想我一个人可以集中精力。我的意思是，我喜欢帮助别人，虽然。我从中得到乐趣。这是一种很好的感觉。

00:51:42.950 --> 00:51:48.540
<v Man>We've got a cancel on our Hazelwood and Rosewood. The member found her keys.
<v Man>我们在Hazelwood和Rosewood的电话取消了。会员找到了她的钥匙。

00:51:48.540 --> 00:52:32.060
<v Kelly Keggers>Cops wave at me when I drive by. Buses stop and let me go by them. It's weird. All city workers are always like, hey, how's it going? Oh, AAA. Hi. You know, waving. And people flag me down a lot when I'm driving. Or if they need a jump-start or whatever, they jump in front of your car with jumper cables. I'm going 40. They fly in front of the truck. You're like, [BRAKING SOUNDS], oh crap. But, no, they treat me really good. They're really nice. I don't get treated like a punk kid, like I usually do.
<v Kelly Keggers>我开车经过时警察对我招手。公交车停下来让我经过。这很奇怪。所有的城市工作人员总是像，嘿，怎么样？哦，AAA。嗨。你知道，招手。当我在开车时，人们经常向我招手示意。或者如果他们需要跳线启动或什么的，他们跳到你的车前拿着跳线。我开40。他们飞到卡车前。你就像，[刹车声]，哦该死。但是，不，他们对我很好。他们真的很友善。我不像平时那样被当作朋克小孩对待。

00:52:32.060 --> 00:53:08.350
<v Kelly Keggers>I jump-started some guy's car on 21st, oh no, it was 22nd street, and there's a big, huge hill. And it was a stick shift, and I told him to keep his foot on the brake. And he didn't, of course. And he was an older guy. And I mean, right as soon as his car started, he took his foot off the brake to get out of the car, and it stalled because it was a stick shift and all that. And it went sailing down the street. And it slammed into a tree and totalled his car. It almost ran me over. I jumped out of the way. I pulled a total, like, Dukes of Hazard.
<v Kelly Keggers>我在21街给某个人的车跳线启动，哦不，那是22街，那里有一个又大又高的坡。它是手动挡的，我告诉他把脚踩在刹车上。当然他没做。他是一个年纪较大的人。我的意思是，就在他的车启动时，他把脚从刹车上拿下来想下车，它熄火了，因为它是手动挡等等。它就向街下滑去。它撞上了一棵树，把他的车撞废了。它差点把我撞死。我跳开了。我完成了一个彻底的，像《危险公爵》一样的动作。

00:53:08.350 --> 00:53:36.830
<v Kelly Keggers>And of course we had to call a tow truck for that. But it wasn't my fault. It was his fault. I advised him not to take his foot off the brake, and blah, blah, blah. But he was old and he was a little grumpy. He's like, I know what to do. His wife started screaming at him. It was really upsetting. I sat with him for a while. He was shaken up. He was scared. He was still in the car. I mean, he tried to get out but he couldn't.
<v Kelly Keggers>当然我们不得不给那个叫拖车。但那不是我的错。是他的错。我建议他不要把脚从刹车上拿下来，等等。但他年纪大，而且有点脾气暴躁。他说，我知道该怎么做。他的妻子开始对他尖叫。那真的很令人不安。我和他坐了一会儿。他被吓坏了。他很害怕。他还在车里。我的意思是，他试图下车但他下不来。

00:53:36.830 --> 00:53:59.300
<v Kelly Keggers>Yeah, that was my big excitement for one day. But everything else is pretty normal. I mean, you get babies locked in cars and stuff. People are freaking out. I try and get there really fast if there's a baby in the car. So I always do that as a priority.
<v Kelly Keggers>是的，那是我的大兴奋一天。但其他一切都很正常。我的意思是，你有锁在车里的婴儿之类的。人们吓坏了。如果车里有婴儿，我会尝试很快赶到那里。所以我总是把那个作为优先事项。

00:53:59.300 --> 00:54:11.080
<v Kelly Keggers>It's cool when you pull into a shopping center, a big parking lot, because you'll always get two or three calls. People will flag you down. And usually they have AAA. And you write them up.
<v Kelly Keggers>当你驶入购物中心、大型停车场时很酷，因为你总会接到两三个电话。人们会向你招手。而且他们通常有AAA。你就给他们写单子。

00:54:11.080 --> 00:54:14.470
<v Man>[UNINTELLIGIBLE] again. [UNINTELLIGIBLE PHRASE].
<v Man>[听不清] 又。 [听不清的短语]。

00:54:14.470 --> 00:54:22.960
<v Kelly Keggers>This parking lot is humongous. Hello. See, you're waiting out front.
<v Kelly Keggers>这个停车场非常巨大。你好。看，你在前面等。

00:54:22.960 --> 00:54:27.610
<v Man>Well, yeah. Got them locked them inside. You know, on the supervisor side.
<v Man>嗯，是的。把它们锁在里面了。你知道，在主管那边。

00:54:27.610 --> 00:55:06.520
<v Kelly Keggers>OK, the supervisor side, all right. I'm totally addicted to that getting paid for job performance. Because that way it doesn't make me resentful of the people I work with if they're not pulling their weight. I just figure, they did 10 calls today, and I did, like, 32 calls. I know that I'm getting paid for all the calls I'm doing. I can't deal with getting pissed off at people I work with. But I like all the guys I work with. They're nice guys. There's one or two other girls that work there.
<v Kelly Keggers>好的，主管那边，好的。我完全沉迷于那种因工作表现而获得报酬。因为那样就不会让我对和我一起工作的人产生怨恨，如果他们没有尽到自己的责任的话。我只是想，他们今天打了10个电话，而我打了大概32个电话。我知道我因为我所做的一切电话而得到报酬。我无法处理对和我一起工作的人感到生气。但我喜欢我一起工作的所有家伙。他们是好人。那里还有一两个其他女孩在工作。

00:55:06.520 --> 00:55:38.680
<v Kelly Keggers>But those guys are cool. And plus, you talk to them. We're all talking about our jobs. And it gives me ideas about-- I like to change jobs a lot. I like to be a total jack of all trades and bounce around. I can only stand a job for, like, a year or two. Like, my next job, I'm going to be a truck driver. I'm going to drive the big rigs. That's my dream, just drive across country completely alone, get my dog and truck, and that's it, just listen to music and just drive.
<v Kelly Keggers>但那些家伙很酷。而且加上，你和他们聊天。我们都在谈论我们的工作。这给了我关于——我喜欢经常换工作。我喜欢成为一个全才，四处跳跃。我只能忍受一份工作大概一年或两年。像，我的下一份工作，我要成为一名卡车司机。我要驾驶大型拖车。那是我的梦想，只是独自驾驶穿越全国，带上我的狗和卡车，就是这样，只是听音乐，只是开车。

00:55:38.680 --> 00:56:35.240
<v Kelly Keggers>This way, right? Third row? One, two-- he's all running. Poor guy.
<v Kelly Keggers>这边，对吧？第三排？一，二——他都在跑。可怜的家伙。

